{"tool_name":"lfa","contents":["bioconda","bioconductor","biotools","oeb"],"identifiers":["biotools:lfa","bioconda:bioconductor-lfa","bioconductor:lfa"],"fetched_metadata":{"bioconda":{"name":"bioconductor-lfa","version":"2.10.0","home":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/html/lfa.html","license":"GPL (>= 3)","summary":"Logistic Factor Analysis for Categorical Data","identifiers":["biotools:lfa","doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/btv641"],"description":"Logistic Factor Analysis is a method for a PCA analogue on Binomial data via estimation of latent structure in the natural parameter.  The main method estimates genetic population structure from genotype data.  There are also methods for estimating individual-specific allele frequencies using the population structure.  Lastly, a structured Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium (HWE) test is developed, which quantifies the goodness of fit of the genotype data to the estimated population structure, via the estimated individual-specific allele frequencies (all of which generalizes traditional HWE tests).","license_file":"/lib/R/share/licenses/GPL-3","build":{"number":0,"rpaths":["lib/R/lib/","lib/"],"run_exports":""},"requirements":{"build":[null,"make"],"host":["r-base","r-corpcor","r-rspectra","libblas","liblapack","zlib","liblzma-devel"],"run":["r-base","r-corpcor","r-rspectra"]},"source_url":["https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/src/contrib/lfa_2.10.0.tar.gz","https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/src/contrib/Archive/lfa/lfa_2.10.0.tar.gz","https://bioarchive.galaxyproject.org/lfa_2.10.0.tar.gz","https://depot.galaxyproject.org/software/bioconductor-lfa/bioconductor-lfa_2.10.0_src_all.tar.gz"],"test":{"commands":["$R -e \"library('lfa')\""]},"parent_recipe":{"name":"bioconductor-lfa","path":"recipes/bioconductor-lfa","version":"1.10.0"}},"biotools":{"id":"lfa","home":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/lfa","license":"GPL-3.0-or-later","summary":"Logistic Factor Analysis is a method for a PCA analogue on Binomial data via estimation of latent structure in the natural parameter.  The main method estimates genetic population structure from genotype data.  There are also methods for estimating individual-specific allele frequencies using the population structure.  Lastly, a structured Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium (HWE) test is developed, which quantifies the goodness of fit of the genotype data to the estimated population structure, via the estimated individual-specific allele frequencies (all of which generalizes traditional HWE tests).","addition_date":"2017-01-17T15:02:50Z","last_update_date":"2026-11-22T22:41:44.273118Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Library"],"version":["2.12.0"],"collections":["BioConductor"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:lfa","credit":[{"name":"Alejandro Ochoa","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer","Maintainer"]},{"name":"John D. Storey","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer"]},{"name":"Minsun Song","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer"]},{"name":"Wei Hao","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["User manual"],"url":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/lfa"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Principal component visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2939"}]}],"name":"lfa","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"bioconductor_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btv641","metadata":{"abstract":"<h4>Motivation</h4>Modern population genetics studies typically involve genome-wide genotyping of individuals from a diverse network of ancestries. An important problem is how to formulate and estimate probabilistic models of observed genotypes that account for complex population structure. The most prominent work on this problem has focused on estimating a model of admixture proportions of ancestral populations for each individual. Here, we instead focus on modeling variation of the genotypes without requiring a higher-level admixture interpretation.<h4>Results</h4>We formulate two general probabilistic models, and we propose computationally efficient algorithms to estimate them. First, we show how principal component analysis can be utilized to estimate a general model that includes the well-known Pritchard-Stephens-Donnelly admixture model as a special case. Noting some drawbacks of this approach, we introduce a new 'logistic factor analysis' framework that seeks to directly model the logit transformation of probabilities underlying observed genotypes in terms of latent variables that capture population structure. We demonstrate these advances on data from the Human Genome Diversity Panel and 1000 Genomes Project, where we are able to identify SNPs that are highly differentiated with respect to structure while making minimal modeling assumptions.<h4>Availability and implementation</h4>A Bioconductor R package called lfa is available at http://www.bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/lfa.html<h4>Contact</h4>jstorey@princeton.edu<h4>Supplementary information</h4>Supplementary data are available at Bioinformatics online.","authors":[{"name":"Hao W"},{"name":"Song M"},{"name":"Storey JD"}],"citationCount":42,"date":"2016-03-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics (Oxford, England)","title":"Probabilistic models of genetic variation in structured populations applied to global human studies."},"pmcid":"PMC4795615","pmid":"26545820"}],"topic":[{"term":"DNA polymorphism","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_2885"},{"term":"Statistics and probability","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_2269"},{"term":"Structure analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0081"}],"validated":1},"bioconductor":{"name":"lfa","version":"2.12.0","license":"GPL (>= 3)","summary":"Logistic Factor Analysis for Categorical Data","description":"Logistic Factor Analysis is a method for a PCA analogue on Binomial data via estimation of latent structure in the natural parameter.  The main method estimates genetic population structure from genotype data.  There are also methods for estimating individual-specific allele frequencies using the population structure.  Lastly, a structured Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium (HWE) test is developed, which quantifies the goodness of fit of the genotype data to the estimated population structure, via the estimated individual-specific allele frequencies (all of which generalizes traditional HWE tests).","home":"https://github.com/StoreyLab/lfa","author":"Wei Hao [aut], Minsun Song [aut], Alejandro Ochoa [aut, cre] (ORCID: <https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4928-3403>), John D. Storey [aut] (ORCID: <https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5992-402X>)","maintainer":"Alejandro Ochoa <alejandro.ochoa@duke.edu>","bioc_views":["DimensionReduction","PrincipalComponent","Regression","SNP","Software"],"git_url":"https://git.bioconductor.org/packages/lfa","last_commit_date":"2026-04-28"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:lfa:1.4.0/cmd/bioconductor.org","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"sourcecode":{"interpreted":true}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"manual":true},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":20,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":2014},{"count":1,"year":2016},{"count":1,"year":2018},{"count":1,"year":2020},{"count":1,"year":2023},{"count":1,"year":2024},{"count":2,"year":2015},{"count":2,"year":2017},{"count":2,"year":2021},{"count":2,"year":2025},{"count":2,"year":2026},{"count":4,"year":2019}],"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btv641","pmcid":"PMC4795615","pmid":"26545820","ref_count":30,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":1978},{"count":1,"year":1995},{"count":1,"year":1998},{"count":1,"year":1999},{"count":1,"year":2000},{"count":1,"year":2001},{"count":1,"year":2006},{"count":1,"year":2007},{"count":1,"year":2008},{"count":1,"year":2015},{"count":2,"year":2009},{"cou